<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">VAL-Bench: Measuring value alignment in Language Models</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Aman Gupta
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>correspondance to <span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ai-research@masterclass.com</span></span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>MasterClass
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>San Francisco, CA, USA
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Denny O’Shea
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>MasterClass
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>San Francisco, CA, USA
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Fazl Barez
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>correspondance to <span id="id2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">fazl@robots.ox.ac.uk</span></span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Oxford
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>WhiteBox &amp; Martian
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used for tasks where outputs shape human decisions, so it is critical to test whether their responses reflect consistent human values. Existing benchmarks mostly track refusals or predefined safety violations, but these only check rule compliance and do not reveal whether a model upholds a coherent value system when facing controversial real-world issues. We introduce the <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">V</span>alue <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AL</span>ignment <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bench</span>mark (<span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VAL-Bench<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">1</span></span>
            
            
            
          <span id="footnote1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">Our benchmark code and data can be found here: </span><a href="https://github.com/amangup/VAL-Bench" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_medium">https://github.com/amangup/VAL-Bench</a><span id="footnote1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">.</span></span></span></span></span>),
which evaluates whether models maintain a stable value stance across paired prompts
that frame opposing sides of public debates. VAL-Bench consists of 115K such pairs from Wikipedia’s controversial sections. A well-aligned model should express similar underlying views regardless of framing, which we measure using an LLM-as-judge to score agreement or divergence between paired responses. Applied across leading open- and closed-source models, the benchmark reveals large variation in alignment and highlights trade-offs between safety strategies (e.g., refusals)
and more expressive value systems. By providing a scalable, reproducible benchmark,
VAL-Bench enables systematic comparison of how reliably LLMs embody human values.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) are now being evaluated and adopted in settings where their outputs shape human decisions, from classroom teaching (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Appel et al. (2025)</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaufman et al. (2025)</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Freeman (2025)</a></cite>) to medical applications (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chatterji et al. (2025)</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Poon et al. (2025)</a></cite>) and public administration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">OECD, 2025</a>)</cite>. Because of this reach, it is not enough for models to avoid clear safety violations; they must also apply human values consistently across contexts. Current safety benchmarks mostly measure toxicity (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hartvigsen et al. (2022)</a></cite>) or high-consensus risk dimensions (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al. (2022)</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2023)</a></cite>), but these only test rule compliance. They do not reveal whether a model upholds a coherent value system when confronted with controversial real-world issues. Misaligned systems risk not only giving unsafe advice but also spreading contradictory or incoherent value judgments, which can undermine trust or even be adopted by users themselves <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barez &amp; Torr, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hubinger et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Denison et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Measuring alignment is difficult.
Existing benchmarks mostly test refusals or predefined safety violations, which check rule compliance. Recent work has highlighted vulnerabilities that persist even after alignment training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hubinger et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Denison et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and introduced benchmarks for specific risks such as data poisoning or reliability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghosh et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, systematic evaluation of whether models apply values consistently across different framings remains missing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce the <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">V</span>alue <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AL</span>ignment <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bench</span>mark (<span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VAL-Bench</span>), the first benchmark that evaluates whether LLMs maintain a stable value stance across prompts framed from differing sides of the same issue. VAL-Bench consists of 115K paired prompts mined from Wikipedia’s controversial sections <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wikimedia Enterprise, 2024</a>)</cite>, spanning politics, civil rights, intellectual property, and more. Using an LLM-as-a-judge to measure agreement between response pairs, we systematically assess models’ value alignment consistency. Our results show large differences between models: Claude models achieve alignment scores almost three times as high as GPT models, with refusal strategies driving much of the variation. This highlights trade-offs between alignment and expressive value systems.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05465v1/VAL-Bench.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/365;" width="548" height="365" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Illustration of how VAL-bench assesses alignment. When asked to support opposing views, misaligned AI systems often yield, whereas aligned systems maintain consistency. Both response pairs shown are generated by recent, popular LLMs.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This design has several advantages that make VAL-Bench distinctive as an alignment benchmark:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Grounded and comprehensive:</span> prompts are drawn from real-world controversial debates across diverse domains, ensuring broad coverage.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Objective and pluralism-friendly:</span> evaluation measures agreement between paired responses rather than judging “desirable” values. Neutral stances count as consistent, and different models can be aligned in themselves even if they disagree with each other.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scalable and updatable:</span> automated construction from Wikipedia makes the dataset large in scale and easy to refresh as societal debates evolve.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Contributions.</span></p>
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VAL-Bench</span>, a scalable benchmark for measuring whether LLMs maintain consistent value stances across opposing framings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct a dataset of 115K paired prompts from Wikipedia’s controversial sections and derive a taxonomy of values expressed in model outputs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We benchmark leading open- and closed-source models, revealing substantial variation in alignment and systematic trade-offs between refusals and value expressivity.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Value Alignment.</span>
Early work on measuring value alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barez &amp; Torr, 2023</a>)</cite> emphasized the importance of testing whether models reflect human values.
Other recent studies have shown that models can exhibit inconsistent or reward-driven behaviors, such as sycophancy or reward tampering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Denison et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, further motivating the need for benchmarks that directly evaluate value consistency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets of Human Values and Alignment.</span>
Recent datasets examine how human values are represented in AI systems.
PRISM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirk et al., 2024</a>)</cite> collects alignment data from participants in 75 countries, highlighting both diversity of perspectives and frequent discussion of controversial topics such as gender, religion, and politics.
Values in the Wild <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> analyzes over 300K human–AI conversations, building a taxonomy of 3,000 values and showing that models often emphasize helpfulness, professionalism, and transparency.
While these works catalogue values demonstrated in general human-AI interactions, VAL-Bench differs by testing whether models <span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">apply values consistently</span> specifically on complex real-world issues.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Safety and Robustness Benchmarks.</span>
Large-scale evaluations such as DecodingTrust <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, HolisticEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and SALAD-bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite> measure toxicity, bias, or refusal rates in LLMs.
These studies highlight the difficulty of robust evaluation, often depending on fixed categories or external APIs.
Domain-specific analyses show similar risks: <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gourabathina et al. (2025)</a></cite> report that subtle changes in tone can alter medical advice, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheung et al. (2025)</a></cite> find that LLMs are far more framing-sensitive than humans in moral dilemmas.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Dataset</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we describe how the <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VAL-bench</span> dataset was created. As mentioned in the abstract, we used the English content of Wikipedia, specifically the structured Wikipedia dataset published by Wikimedia on Hugging Face (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wikimedia Enterprise (2024)</a></cite>. We followed the following steps to get 114,745 pairs of prompts:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We filtered the Wikipedia sections using common terms used to designate controversial sections in Wikipedia, such as <span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Criticism</span> and <span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Scandals</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We prompted open-weights model <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemma-3-27B-it</span> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team (2025)</a></cite>) to assess the <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">awareness</span> of the issue, i.e., relevance of the issue to the society. We used that to filter out sections that didn’t represent a divergent issue.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further prompted <span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemma-3-27B-it</span> to extract the pair of abductive reasoning prompts from the text of a single controversial section in Wikipedia, each starting with <span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Explain why</span>. Each pair of prompts is referred to as <span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for prompt</span> and <span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">against prompt</span>; there is no moral <span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">direction</span> attached to this naming.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The prompts used are shared in the appendix.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Description of issues</h3>

<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Issue awareness (as judged by LLM). Illustrates the scale and diversity of issue types. </figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Issue Awareness</span></th>
