<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Values represented in the prompts</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The prompts <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inspire</span> certain values in the response; for example, the prompts related to sweatshops’ economic impact in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ VAL-Bench: Measuring value alignment in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> can inspire <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">equality</span> (<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for prompt</span>) or <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pragmatism</span> (<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">against prompt</span>). We use LLMs to extract over 34,000 values from 22,950 prompts in the test split, and then reduce the set of values to 1,000 values using an encoder model to compute representations and using K-means clustering. As an illustration of the outcome, the values associated with the sweatshops issue include <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">workers’ rights</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inequality minimization</span> (<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for prompt</span>), and <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">utilitarianism</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">labor devaluation</span> (<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">against prompt</span>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The top 10 values extracted are shown in Table <a href="#S3.T2.st2" title="In Table 2 ‣ 3.1 Description of issues ‣ 3 Dataset ‣ VAL-Bench: Measuring value alignment in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a>. The list includes values that most people would consider <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">morally good</span> (<span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">accountability</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">prioritizing justice</span>), <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">morally bad</span> (<span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">evading accountability</span>), or <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">morally ambiguous</span> (<span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tradition prioritization</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">legal formalism</span>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Evaluation</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formulate the process of evaluation as follows. The benchmark dataset <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathbb{D}}=\{(c_{i},p_{i}^{+},p_{i}^{-})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathbb{D}}=\{(c_{i},p_{i}^{+},p_{i}^{-})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> issues described with an issue description <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and paired prompts representing the <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for prompt</span> and <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">against prompt</span>. We use an LM <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate the response dataset <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathbb{G}}_{\mathcal{M}}=\{\mathcal{M}(p_{i}^{+}),\mathcal{M}(p_{i}^{-})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathbb{G}}_{\mathcal{M}}=\{\mathcal{M}(p_{i}^{+}),\mathcal{M}(p_{i}^{-})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. To measure alignment, we use an evaluator <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{E}:(c_{i},p_{i}^{+},r_{i}^{+},p_{i}^{-},r_{i}^{-})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{E}:(c_{i},p_{i}^{+},r_{i}^{+},p_{i}^{-},r_{i}^{-})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mapsto\bigl(\sigma_{i},\psi(r_{i}^{+}),\nu(r_{i}^{+}),\psi(r_{i}^{-}),\nu(r_{i}^{-})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mapsto\bigl(\sigma_{i},\psi(r_{i}^{+}),\nu(r_{i}^{+}),\psi(r_{i}^{-}),\nu(r_{i}^{-})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\in\{-2,-1,0,1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\in\{-2,-1,0,1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\psi(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\psi(r)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if $r$ is a refusal response},\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mi>r</mi><mtext> is a refusal response</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if $r$ is a refusal response},\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nu(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nu(r)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if $r$ is a no-information response},\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mi>r</mi><mtext> is a no-information response</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\begin{cases}1,&amp;\text{if $r$ is a no-information response},\\
0,&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Where <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}=-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}=-2</annotation></semantics></math> implies <span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">complete disagreement</span> and <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}=2</annotation></semantics></math> implies <span id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">complete agreement</span> between generated responses. The <span id="S4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PAC</span> alignment scores dataset <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathbb{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathbb{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathbb{S}}=\{\,s_{i}\,\}_{i=1}^{N},\quad s_{i}=\begin{cases}\sigma_{i},&amp;\text{if }\psi(r_{i}^{+})=0\text{ and }\psi(r_{i}^{-})=0,\\[6.0pt]
0,&amp;\text{if }\psi(r_{i}^{+})+\psi(r_{i}^{-})=1,\\[6.0pt]
2,&amp;\text{if }\psi(r_{i}^{+})+\psi(r_{i}^{-})=2.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝕊</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathbb{S}}=\{\,s_{i}\,\}_{i=1}^{N},\quad s_{i}=\begin{cases}\sigma_{i},&amp;\text{if }\psi(r_{i}^{+})=0\text{ and }\psi(r_{i}^{-})=0,\\[6.0pt]
0,&amp;\text{if }\psi(r_{i}^{+})+\psi(r_{i}^{-})=1,\\[6.0pt]
2,&amp;\text{if }\psi(r_{i}^{+})+\psi(r_{i}^{-})=2.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The score definition in equation <a href="#S4.E5" title="In 4 Evaluation ‣ VAL-Bench: Measuring value alignment in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> recognizes that refusals (responses stating that they can’t provide an opinion or take a stance) are commonly used for achieving alignment. For pairs with no-information responses (responses stating that there is insufficient information), we use the evaluator computed score <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Metrics</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>airwise <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>lignment <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>onsistency (<span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PAC</span>) as our main metric. For each input, we prompt the model twice: once with a “for” framing and once with an “against” framing. If the model answers consistently (e.g., supporting one side and opposing the other), it scores high. If the answers contradict each other or flip position, it scores low. This approach fits value alignment because there is often no single “correct” answer to compare against, but on the other hand, it’s possible for a model to score high while prioritizing values that many regard as undesirable. PAC works across many domains, avoids cherry-picking single outputs, and produces a clear score between 0 and 100 that can be calibrated across evaluators.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.1 Metrics ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ VAL-Bench: Measuring value alignment in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> describes the notation for all the metrics we list in the results section. We do not define a preference for ideal <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NINF</span> rate, since both overuse (becoming a substitute for refusals) and underuse (causing hallucinations) of no-information responses are problematic. Instead, we report it as an auxiliary metric, enabling future work to interpret its desirability in context.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Definition and description of metric symbols used to form a comprehensive picture of alignment and its tradeoff with expressivity. Arrows denote the preferred direction of performance.</figcaption>
