<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.2 Evaluator calibration ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ VAL-Bench: Measuring value alignment in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> contains the description of the calibration datasets alongside measured <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PAC</span> and <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">REF</span> scores by our chosen evaluator model and prompt (denoted by <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\widehat{PAC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐏𝐀𝐂</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\widehat{PAC}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\widehat{REF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐑𝐄𝐅</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\widehat{REF}}</annotation></semantics></math>), and by humans for whom we sampled 50 samples from each of the calibration datasets for a total of 200 human annotations (denoted by <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PAC*</span> and <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">REF*</span>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Evaluator ablations</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We ran two ablations to ensure the accuracy of evaluation scores. Since the ordering of information in the input to LLMs matters, we conducted the evaluation with the paired responses presented in reverse order. To measure the deviation, we computed RMSE using both sets (normal and reversed orderings) of <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PAC</span>, <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">REF</span>, and <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NINF</span> metrics, resulting in values of <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.28</span>, <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.30</span>, and <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.57</span>, respectively. We report the mean of these two runs for each model in the results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In another ablation, we generated paraphrased versions of the responses in the <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">unaligned-0</span> and <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">aligned-100</span> calibration datasets (using an LLM instructed to make the responses concise and increase readability), and recomputed the metrics. This resulted in a <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PAC</span> score <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">increase</span> of <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3.71</span> and <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3.63</span> for <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">unaligned-0</span> and <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">aligned-100</span> datasets, respectively. This indicates that the evaluator is relatively insensitive to changes in the style of expression.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Chat model alignment scores sorted by alignment (metric definitions can be seen in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.1 Metrics ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ VAL-Bench: Measuring value alignment in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). The alignment-expressivity tradeoff is clearly demonstrated in these results. 
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:3.0pt;">
Arrows in the column headers denote the preferred direction of performance. Bold indicates best scores; underline indicates worst scores in each column.</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
