</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Thinking and Chat models</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate a gamut of LLMs accessible through API or open weights. Ever since the launch of OpenAI’s o1 (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI et al. (2024)</a></cite>), models trained with RLVR (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al. (2025)</a></cite>) to output a detailed chain-of-thought (CoT) before the final response have shown incredible performance. We measure whether these models can utilize CoT to improve value alignment. We refer to these models as <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reasoning</span> or <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">thinking</span> models, and the “regular” models as <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">chat</span> models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Results</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Chat models</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.3 Evaluator ablations ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ VAL-Bench: Measuring value alignment in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, Claude models score the highest on alignment, but also have correspondingly higher refusal rates and no-information response rates (particularly for <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">claude-haiku-3.5</span>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">OpenAI’s models and most open-weights models typically score far lower on the alignment leaderboard. <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">qwen3-235B-instruct-2507</span> achieves the highest <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PAC</span> score among non-Claude models, while having relatively low refusal and no-information response rates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also include a pretrained only model <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">glm-4.5-air-base</span> in the list. This is the model we used for generating the <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">unaligned-0</span> calibration dataset. As one might expect, it invariably provided the abductive reasoning expected by the prompts, leading to the lowest alignment score.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Thinking models</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the ability to spend a considerable number of tokens dedicated towards CoT, models may be expected to achieve higher alignment. The results (shown in Table <a href="#S5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 5.2 Thinking models ‣ 5 Results ‣ VAL-Bench: Measuring value alignment in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) don’t show a consistent pattern, however. Among the models tested, only <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">qwen3</span>’s thinking models show a meaningful improvement. OpenAI’s <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">o4-mini</span> performs the worst of any model in the gpt-4 series.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Thinking model alignment score sorted by alignment (metric definitions can be seen in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.1 Metrics ‣ 4 Evaluation ‣ VAL-Bench: Measuring value alignment in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). Qwen3 thinking models improve alignment compared to their non-thinking counterparts. 
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:3.0pt;">
Arrows in the column headers denote the preferred direction of performance. Bold indicates best scores; underline indicates worst scores in each column.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T6.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T6.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T6.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S5.T6.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S5.T6.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T6.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></span>
</span></th>
