<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Values demonstrated in responses</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using the value annotations of the prompts (Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Values represented in the prompts ‣ 3 Dataset ‣ VAL-Bench: Measuring value alignment in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), we directly analyze the values models <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">demonstrate</span>. Specifically, we use an LLM to annotate each response with the values it demonstrates from among those <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">inspired</span> by the prompt. Remember, this value expectation is implicit - the models demonstrate these values naturally.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To understand a model’s <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">value system</span>, we measure <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deviation</span> in the frequency distribution of <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">expected values</span> (extracted from prompts) and <span id="S5.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">demonstrated values</span>, using the established value taxonomy. The expected distribution, derived from both for and against prompts, represents an inconsistent value system, and high deviation indicates a well-defined value system. We plot the Pearson residuals (Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5.3 Values demonstrated in responses ‣ 5 Results ‣ VAL-Bench: Measuring value alignment in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) for the ten highest residual values (denoting over-representation) and the ten least residual values (denoting under-representation).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Even with <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">glm-4.5-air-base</span>, a pretrained model with no post-training, the responses show a shift towards many morally good values (<span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">prioritizing accountability</span>, <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">prioritizing equity</span>) and against many morally bad values (<span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">evading accountability</span>, <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">marginalization</span>). But, the list of over-represented values includes some morally ambiguous ones, like <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pragmatism over principle</span> and <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">utilitarianism</span>. <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">gpt-5-chat</span>’s ten highest residual values also include <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pragmatism over principle</span>. <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">claude-opus-4.1</span> seems to have the largest absolute residuals among the three, while having subtly different values in the top ten highest/least residuals.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.05465v1/response_value_residuals-glm-4-5-air-base.png" id="S5.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:548/731;" width="548" height="731" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>GLM-4.5 Air Base 
<br class="ltx_break ltx_centering"><math id="S5.F2.sf1.m3" class="ltx_centering" alttext="VDR=0.79" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.79</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">VDR=0.79</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.F2.sf1.m4" class="ltx_centering" alttext="\tilde{\chi}^{2}=3.41" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>3.41</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\chi}^{2}=3.41</annotation></semantics></math> </figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.05465v1/response_value_residuals-gpt-5-chat.png" id="S5.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:548/731;" width="548" height="731" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>GPT 5 Chat 
<br class="ltx_break ltx_centering"><math id="S5.F2.sf2.m3" class="ltx_centering" alttext="VDR=0.78" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.78</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">VDR=0.78</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.F2.sf2.m4" class="ltx_centering" alttext="\tilde{\chi}^{2}=4.47" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4.47</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\chi}^{2}=4.47</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F2.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.05465v1/response_value_residuals-claude-opus-4-1.png" id="S5.F2.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:548/731;" width="548" height="731" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>Claude Opus 4.1 
<br class="ltx_break ltx_centering"><math id="S5.F2.sf3.m3" class="ltx_centering" alttext="VDR=0.55" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.55</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">VDR=0.55</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.F2.sf3.m4" class="ltx_centering" alttext="\tilde{\chi}^{2}=8.38" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>8.38</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\chi}^{2}=8.38</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Values demonstrated by LLM responses compared to expected values from the prompts. Bars represent Pearson residuals; positive residuals indicate over-representation and negative residuals indicate under-representation. (<math id="S5.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>) shows actual
counts of responses demonstrating each value. 
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:3.0pt;">
<math id="S5.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="VDR" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">VDR</annotation></semantics></math> is the Value Demonstration Rate, measuring value expressivity.
<math id="S5.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\chi}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\chi}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Reduced <math id="S5.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\chi}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\chi}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> Statistic; indicating value alignment. These metrics illustrate the alignment-expressivity tradeoff emerging from direct value analysis.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To summarize the trend, we define <span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">value demonstration rate</span> (<math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="VDR" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">VDR</annotation></semantics></math>) as the ratio of the sum of frequencies of demonstrated values compared to the sum of frequencies of expected values, and use <span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">reduced <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>χ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> statistic</span> (<math id="S5.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\chi}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>χ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\chi}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) as an aggregate measure of the deviation. Both are shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5.3 Values demonstrated in responses ‣ 5 Results ‣ VAL-Bench: Measuring value alignment in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, revealing the alignment-expressivity tradeoff.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Correlations with issue awareness</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We expect model alignment to differ depending on the kind of issue that’s being prompted. We measured how alignment rates (<span id="S5.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PAC</span>), refusal rates (<span id="S5.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">REF</span>), and no-information response rates (<span id="S5.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NINF</span>) vary with <span id="S5.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Issue awareness</span>. The results are shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.4 Correlations with issue awareness ‣ 5 Results ‣ VAL-Bench: Measuring value alignment in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The correlations are quite clear: <span id="S5.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">NINF</span> shows a negative correlation, matching our intuition that as issue awareness increases, models avoid the no-information response. Claude models’ propensity to use no-information responses was also observed in the SimpleQA benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.05465v1/ninf_issue_awareness.png" id="S5.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:548/470;" width="548" height="470" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>No-info rate (<math id="S5.F3.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\rho}=-0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\rho}=-0.99</annotation></semantics></math>)</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.05465v1/ref_issue_awareness.png" id="S5.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:548/470;" width="548" height="470" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Refusal Rate (<math id="S5.F3.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\rho}=0.96" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0.96</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\rho}=0.96</annotation></semantics></math>)</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S5.F3.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.05465v1/pac_issue_awareness.png" id="S5.F3.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:548/470;" width="548" height="470" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>Alignment Score (<math id="S5.F3.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\rho}=0.89" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>0.89</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\rho}=0.89</annotation></semantics></math>)</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Metrics vs Issue awareness. We see clear correlations with all 3 primary metrics.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PAC</span> and <span id="S5.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">REF</span> show positive correlations. While refusal rates increase with issue awareness, the positive correlation with alignment itself exists independently of refusal rates. Both <span id="S5.SS4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">gpt-5-chat</span> and <span id="S5.SS4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">deepseek-r1</span> show that trend while having negligible refusal rates. It <span id="S5.SS4.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">suggests</span> a bias toward over-prioritizing prominent issues in datasets used in alignment training of these models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our VAL-Bench results highlight the dependence of alignment on the internal choices of each frontier model builder. OpenAI models consistently score low while avoiding refusals. Claude shows significantly higher alignment alonside higher refusal or no-information response rates. This dichotomy clearly illustrates the alignment-expressivity tradeoff, or <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">alignment tax</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Qwen3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> appears to pursue alignment through explicit opinions with low refusal rates. Given AI alignment’s societal importance and varying interpretations among frontier labs, we need a common, open process for creating human-annotated alignment datasets.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Training for value alignment</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All LLMs go through a large-scale <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">pretraining</span> phase with the cross-entropy objective that can be derived as the Maximum Likelihood Estimator. Modern LLMs are very effective at reducing this loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, thereby modeling the broad distribution of viewpoints present in their training data, leading to unaligned models as illustrated by <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">glm-4.5-air-base</span>’s <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PAC</span> score. It is impractical to “clean” the extraordinarily large pretraining datasets (up to 36T tokens, as per <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025)</a></cite>) to encode a consistent value system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Alignment is commonly pursued through RL methods like RLHF (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al. (2022)</a></cite>) or preference-based methods like DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Im &amp; Li (2024)</a></cite> shows that the effectiveness of DPO depends heavily on large, diverse datasets, which helps explain why organizations invest in costly human feedback datasets (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. (2023)</a></cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>)</cite>). These methods require extensive datasets demonstrating correct behavior across many situations, and our results highlight the challenge of scaling value alignment while maintaining expressivity.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.1.1 </span>Belief consistency in language model training</h4>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In humans, beliefs often underpin values <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schwartz et al., 2012</a>)</cite>. Thus, achieving <span id="S6.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">belief consistency</span> also entails, at least in part, achieving value consistency. A training algorithm that is compatible with belief revision while maintaining consistency, like the AGM belief revision theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alchourrón et al., 1985</a>)</cite>, could, in principle, reduce the need for large and diverse datasets with explicit examples of alignment behavior; the consistent belief system would be formed during the self-supervised training phase itself. The variation and inconsistency of beliefs in pretraining datasets could actually inform the belief entrenchment (e.g., “vaccines are good for society” is strongly held, whereas “technology is an inherent good” is weakly held), as proposed in the AGM theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Makinson &amp; Gärdenfors, 2015</a>)</cite>. However, currently, we lack operators that measure logical relationships like contradiction or consequence in neural representation spaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Garcez &amp; Lamb, 2023</a>)</cite>, preventing direct implementation of such a method.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VAL-Bench</span>, a large-scale benchmark testing whether LLMs demonstrate consistent values across prompts with opposing framings of controversial issues. Unlike prior benchmarks focused on predefined safety violations like toxicity, VAL-Bench directly measures value expression coherence. Leading open and closed-weights models show substantial variation in value alignment consistency; models relying heavily on refusals often avoid expressing values, while more expressive models risk inconsistency across framings. These trade-offs highlight a core challenge in alignment: safety mechanisms can mask, but not resolve, whether models embody stable value systems. By grounding evaluation in real-world controversies and enabling scalable automated assessment, VAL-Bench provides a reproducible tool for tracking value alignment progress and building models with consistent value systems. Future work could extend VAL-Bench beyond English Wikipedia, incorporate more universal value taxonomies, and investigate mechanistic causes of inconsistency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Ethics Statements</h2>

<section id="Sx1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Disclosure of LLM use</h3>

<div id="Sx1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We utilize OpenAI ChatGPT, mainly GPT-5 and sometimes GPT-4o, and Anthropic Claude Sonnet 4 to (1) aid and polish writing, and (2) retrieve and discover related literature. We manually verify all related works originally suggested by ChatGPT or Claude. No full paragraphs are completely generated by these LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Ethics Statement</h3>

<div id="Sx1.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose VAL-Bench to improve alignment and safety evaluation of language models. We do not anticipate direct negative ethical impacts beyond the usual risks of benchmark misuse, which we mitigate by open release and clear intended-use documentation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Reproducibility Statement</h3>

<div id="Sx1.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure the reproducibility of our results, we made our data, code and benchmark publically available.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx1.SSx3.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Acknowledgments</h4>

<div id="Sx1.SSx3.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.SSx3.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Special thanks to our colleagues Yang Zhang, April Xu (MasterClass) as well as Nazneen Rajani (Collinear.ai) for reviewing draft versions of the paper. We appreciate our partner Innodata’s expediency in completing the human annotations for evaluator calibration.</p>
</div>
</section>
